  "slug": "mitochondrial-resource-16ed",
  "summary": "Recent methods prioritize noncoding variants via unsupervised scoring, LD-mismatch-robust fine-mapping, and causal network inference from trios.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is computational prioritization of variants with regulatory or causal relevance beyond simple association. eQIF applies unsupervised isolation forests to combine Enformer predictions into a single gene-regulatory score per common variant, targeting eQTL and GWAS variants. RSparsePro addresses a practical limitation of fine-mapping: it uses a probabilistic graphical model with variational inference that remains robust to LD reference mismatch, with improved credible-set power. XGBoost-SHAP applied to snATAC-seq from iPSC-derived pancreatic progenitors flags noncoding SNPs predicted to affect HNF4A binding, outperforming linear models. MRGN is methodologically distinct, extending regression-based Mendelian randomization to infer causal genomic networks from trios under high-dimensional confounders. What is new is the emphasis on robustness to reference and confounding assumptions, alongside label-free scoring of regulatory effects.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-09"
}
